WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.820
Welcome back.

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:02.600
We have learned to draw forces.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.360
Now let us use those diagrams to solve richer problems, without collecting a

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.720
mountain of unrelated formulas.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.540
First, momentum.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.280
For a constant mass, momentum is mass times velocity.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.040
It is a vector, so direction matters.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.800
Impulse tells us how much momentum changes.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.540
For a constant force, impulse is force times the time interval.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.340
For a changing force, it is the signed area under the force versus time graph.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.940
Imagine a two kilogram ball moving right at three meters per second.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.320
After a hit, it moves left at two meters per second.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.120
Choose right as positive.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.000
What is its change in momentum?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:41.260
Take a moment.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.520
Initial momentum is plus six.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.600
Final momentum is minus four.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.400
Final minus initial gives minus ten kilogram meters per second, or minus ten

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.000
newton seconds.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:56.260
If the hit lasts zero point two seconds, the average net force is minus fifty

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:56.600
newtons.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.080
Subtracting speeds would miss the reversal.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.720
Now look at a triangular net force pulse.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.960
Its base is zero point four seconds and its peak is twenty newtons.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.320
The area is one half times base times height, or four newton seconds.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.604
For a half kilogram body, this gives an eight meter per second change in

00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:15.040
velocity.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.160
Finally, choose a whole system.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.880
Internal interaction forces cancel in its total momentum equation.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.740
When the net external impulse is zero, total momentum stays constant, although

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.340
each object's momentum can change.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.780
A short collision may let us neglect a small external impulse, but we must

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.720
state that approximation.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:38.220
In JEE, first decide which body or system your momentum equation describes.

00:01:38.856 --> 00:01:42.236
Let us pause the motion and study a knot in equilibrium.

00:01:43.156 --> 00:01:46.016
A light knot carries a downward load of one hundred newtons.

00:01:46.936 --> 00:01:48.616
One rope pulls it horizontally left.

00:01:49.616 --> 00:01:52.456
Another pulls up and right at thirty degrees above the horizontal.

00:01:53.256 --> 00:01:57.336
The ropes are taut, and we treat the knot as a particle with negligible mass.

00:01:57.916 --> 00:01:59.516
Which rope supports the vertical load?

00:02:00.536 --> 00:02:01.896
Picture the arrows before answering.

00:02:02.556 --> 00:02:04.576
Only the inclined rope has an upward component.

00:02:05.376 --> 00:02:06.316
Call its tension T.

00:02:06.776 --> 00:02:11.258
Its upward component is T sine thirty degrees, and its rightward component is

00:02:11.258 --> 00:02:11.316
T

00:02:11.316 --> 00:02:12.336
cosine thirty degrees.

00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:17.056
Vertical equilibrium says T sine thirty equals one hundred.

00:02:17.056 --> 00:02:20.056
Sine thirty is one half, so T is two hundred newtons.

00:02:20.836 --> 00:02:23.216
The horizontal rope must balance the rightward component.

00:02:23.816 --> 00:02:27.601
Its tension is two hundred times cosine thirty, or one hundred times the

00:02:27.601 --> 00:02:27.916
square

00:02:27.916 --> 00:02:31.156
root of three newtons, about one hundred seventy three point two.

00:02:31.816 --> 00:02:32.796
Notice something interesting.

00:02:33.256 --> 00:02:35.076
The inclined tension exceeds the weight.

00:02:35.476 --> 00:02:38.756
That is not a mistake: only part of this tension acts upward.

00:02:39.816 --> 00:02:41.036
Try changing the angle in your mind.

00:02:41.596 --> 00:02:45.256
A more nearly horizontal supporting rope needs a larger tension to provide the

00:02:45.256 --> 00:02:46.296
same vertical component.

00:02:47.296 --> 00:02:51.436
For concurrent forces on a particle, setting both component sums to zero gives

00:02:51.436 --> 00:02:51.936
equilibrium.

00:02:52.716 --> 00:02:56.956
For an extended rigid body, zero net force alone is not enough; torque must

00:02:56.956 --> 00:02:57.676
balance too.

00:02:58.136 --> 00:02:59.276
We study that in rotation.

00:02:59.936 --> 00:03:03.536
In an exam, mark whether an angle is measured from the horizontal or vertical.

00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:07.756
Swapping sine and cosine without checking the drawing is a very avoidable lost

00:03:07.756 --> 00:03:08.036
mark.

00:03:08.756 --> 00:03:11.876
Your diagram is doing the thinking before your calculator does the arithmetic.

00:03:12.456 --> 00:03:16.758
Here is an Atwood machine: two hanging masses connected by a rope over a

00:03:16.758 --> 00:03:17.176
pulley.

00:03:17.636 --> 00:03:21.896
Let the masses be three kilograms and two kilograms, and use g equal to ten

00:03:21.896 --> 00:03:22.916
meters per second squared.

00:03:23.896 --> 00:03:26.396
The rope is light, taut and inextensible.

00:03:27.196 --> 00:03:29.036
The pulley is massless and frictionless.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:32.236
These assumptions are part of the problem, not decorations.

00:03:33.096 --> 00:03:35.996
The rope constraint makes the acceleration magnitudes equal.

00:03:36.796 --> 00:03:39.136
One mass moves downward while the other moves upward.

00:03:39.756 --> 00:03:43.656
Our ideal pulley and rope also make the tension equal on both sides.

00:03:44.796 --> 00:03:46.436
Draw two separate free body diagrams.

00:03:47.376 --> 00:03:52.076
On the heavier mass, choose downward as positive: thirty minus T equals three

00:03:52.076 --> 00:03:52.596
a.

00:03:52.596 --> 00:03:57.956
On the lighter mass, choose upward as positive: T minus twenty equals two a.

00:03:57.956 --> 00:04:00.976
We have chosen different positive directions because the bodies move

00:04:00.976 --> 00:04:01.476
oppositely.

00:04:02.436 --> 00:04:03.096
Add the equations.

00:04:03.796 --> 00:04:07.036
Tension disappears, leaving ten equals five a.

00:04:07.036 --> 00:04:09.256
The acceleration is two meters per second squared.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:11.276
Now predict the tension.

00:04:11.936 --> 00:04:13.716
Is it automatically the weight of either mass?

00:04:14.696 --> 00:04:15.336
Think for a moment.

00:04:15.936 --> 00:04:18.036
No, because both masses accelerate.

00:04:19.036 --> 00:04:22.616
Substitute into the lighter mass equation: T minus twenty equals four.

00:04:23.376 --> 00:04:24.676
T is twenty four newtons.

00:04:25.656 --> 00:04:29.811
Check the heavier mass too: thirty minus twenty four equals six, which is

00:04:29.811 --> 00:04:30.096
three

00:04:30.096 --> 00:04:30.656
times two.

00:04:31.416 --> 00:04:32.596
Both diagrams agree.

00:04:32.596 --> 00:04:37.516
Adding equations is useful for finding acceleration; returning to one body

00:04:37.516 --> 00:04:38.136
gives tension.

00:04:39.016 --> 00:04:42.716
If the pulley has rotational inertia, its two tensions need not be equal.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:47.376
If a rope goes slack, the common acceleration constraint stops applying.

00:04:48.556 --> 00:04:52.656
JEE connected body problems become manageable when you separate actual forces

00:04:52.656 --> 00:04:54.826
from the geometric constraint connecting the bodies.

00:04:55.056 --> 00:04:58.156
Friction deserves a decision, not just a substitution.

00:04:58.916 --> 00:05:01.996
Take a four kilogram block on a level floor, initially at rest.

00:05:02.716 --> 00:05:06.836
Use g equal to ten, a static coefficient of zero point five, and a kinetic

00:05:06.836 --> 00:05:08.416
coefficient of zero point three.

00:05:08.976 --> 00:05:12.716
With no other vertical forces, the normal reaction is forty newtons.

00:05:12.716 --> 00:05:16.076
The maximum static friction is twenty newtons.

00:05:16.076 --> 00:05:17.956
Apply an eighteen newton horizontal push.

00:05:18.616 --> 00:05:20.156
Does friction become twenty?

00:05:20.156 --> 00:05:21.016
Have a little think.

00:05:21.016 --> 00:05:21.696
No.

00:05:22.236 --> 00:05:26.396
It adjusts to eighteen newtons, opposing the tendency to slide, and the block

00:05:26.396 --> 00:05:27.176
remains at rest.

00:05:27.976 --> 00:05:29.896
Now apply twenty four newtons.

00:05:29.896 --> 00:05:31.556
Static friction cannot maintain rest.

00:05:32.196 --> 00:05:36.276
Once sliding begins, the kinetic friction magnitude is twelve newtons.

00:05:36.276 --> 00:05:40.536
The net horizontal force is twelve, so acceleration is three meters per second

00:05:40.536 --> 00:05:40.856
squared.

00:05:41.496 --> 00:05:45.336
The coefficient tells us a limit or a sliding model; it does not replace

00:05:45.336 --> 00:05:46.996
identifying the situation.

00:05:46.996 --> 00:05:48.636
What if the pull is angled upward?

00:05:49.356 --> 00:05:52.551
Its vertical component reduces the normal reaction while contact is

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:53.076
maintained.

00:05:53.916 --> 00:05:56.236
Recalculate the normal force before calculating friction.

00:05:57.016 --> 00:06:00.996
On a stationary block on a simple incline, the required friction is m g sine

00:06:00.996 --> 00:06:04.096
theta and the normal reaction is m g cosine theta.

00:06:04.796 --> 00:06:08.436
Rest is possible when tangent theta is no greater than the static coefficient.

00:06:09.096 --> 00:06:10.816
Now distinguish sliding from rolling.

00:06:11.436 --> 00:06:14.972
In ideal pure rolling, the contact point does not slip relative to the

00:06:14.972 --> 00:06:15.376
surface.

00:06:15.376 --> 00:06:19.476
Static friction may act, but it is not automatically at its limiting value.

00:06:20.096 --> 00:06:23.916
A freely rolling wheel on an ideal horizontal surface can have zero friction.

00:06:24.716 --> 00:06:28.516
Real rolling resistance comes from effects such as deformation and is not

00:06:28.516 --> 00:06:30.016
generally the kinetic friction formula.

00:06:30.716 --> 00:06:35.601
For JEE, name the contact, find its slipping tendency, and only then choose

00:06:35.601 --> 00:06:35.796
the

00:06:35.796 --> 00:06:36.136
friction equation.

00:06:36.408 --> 00:06:39.588
A car turns left on a level road at constant speed.

00:06:40.228 --> 00:06:41.468
Is its acceleration zero?

00:06:42.328 --> 00:06:45.708
Its speed stays constant, but its velocity direction changes.

00:06:46.328 --> 00:06:49.608
It needs an inward acceleration of v squared divided by r.

00:06:50.368 --> 00:06:52.868
Now ask which real force supplies that acceleration.

00:06:53.528 --> 00:06:57.928
In a simplified level road model, static friction between the tires and road

00:06:57.928 --> 00:06:59.808
supplies the horizontal inward force.

00:07:00.568 --> 00:07:02.488
Weight and the normal reaction balance vertically.

00:07:03.108 --> 00:07:05.908
There is no separate force to add called centripetal force.

00:07:05.908 --> 00:07:09.328
That phrase describes the job done by the inward net force.

00:07:09.868 --> 00:07:12.848
With no other vertical forces, N equals m g.

00:07:13.448 --> 00:07:16.448
The required friction is m v squared over r.

00:07:17.008 --> 00:07:19.888
Its available maximum is mu s times m g.

00:07:20.488 --> 00:07:26.468
The no skid condition is therefore m v squared over r no greater than mu s m

00:07:26.668 --> 00:07:26.988
g.

00:07:27.628 --> 00:07:32.868
Mass cancels, giving a maximum speed equal to the square root of mu s r g.

00:07:33.348 --> 00:07:38.208
Try our example: coefficient zero point five, turn radius twenty meters, and g

00:07:38.208 --> 00:07:38.928
equal to ten.

00:07:39.628 --> 00:07:41.528
Under the square root we have one hundred.

00:07:41.528 --> 00:07:43.788
The maximum speed is ten meters per second.

00:07:44.348 --> 00:07:47.528
At six meters per second, is friction already at its maximum?

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:48.508
Pause.

00:07:49.008 --> 00:07:49.308
No.

00:07:49.828 --> 00:07:53.668
For a one thousand kilogram car it is one thousand times thirty six divided by

00:07:53.668 --> 00:07:57.388
twenty, or eighteen hundred newtons, below the five thousand newton limit.

00:07:57.628 --> 00:08:02.168
For this calculation we neglect air forces, road irregularities and vehicle

00:08:02.168 --> 00:08:02.568
details.

00:08:03.168 --> 00:08:05.288
It is a physics model, not driving advice.

00:08:06.008 --> 00:08:09.008
Smaller radius means a tighter turn and a lower limiting speed.

00:08:09.608 --> 00:08:13.557
When you see a circular motion question, draw the actual forces first and

00:08:13.557 --> 00:08:13.828
write

00:08:13.828 --> 00:08:17.448
their inward components equal to m v squared over r.

00:08:17.568 --> 00:08:20.848
A banked road tilts upward toward the outside of a turn.

00:08:21.588 --> 00:08:25.668
This tilts the normal reaction inward, so part of that force can turn the car

00:08:25.668 --> 00:08:26.788
even without friction.

00:08:27.948 --> 00:08:32.528
Draw a cross section: inward is horizontal toward the center, while weight

00:08:32.528 --> 00:08:33.828
remains vertically downward.

00:08:34.408 --> 00:08:36.588
Let theta be the road's angle above horizontal.

00:08:37.268 --> 00:08:40.168
The normal reaction makes the same angle with the vertical.

00:08:40.708 --> 00:08:45.168
In the ideal frictionless model, its vertical component is N cosine theta.

00:08:45.168 --> 00:08:49.488
That balances m g because the car has no vertical acceleration.

00:08:50.528 --> 00:08:52.728
Its inward component is N sine theta.

00:08:53.308 --> 00:08:54.908
That equals m v squared over r.

00:08:55.588 --> 00:08:57.748
Divide the inward equation by the vertical equation.

00:08:58.808 --> 00:09:00.188
Both N and mass disappear.

00:09:01.028 --> 00:09:03.928
Tangent theta equals v squared divided by r g.

00:09:03.928 --> 00:09:07.148
So the ideal speed is the square root of r g tangent theta.

00:09:08.088 --> 00:09:11.948
Take a twenty meter radius, a forty five degree bank and g equal to ten.

00:09:12.508 --> 00:09:16.708
Tangent forty five equals one, giving ten times the square root of two meters

00:09:16.708 --> 00:09:19.908
per second, about fourteen point one four.

00:09:19.908 --> 00:09:23.808
This is the speed requiring no friction in our model, not a universal maximum.

00:09:24.548 --> 00:09:25.848
What happens below that speed?

00:09:26.548 --> 00:09:28.708
Think about a nearly stationary car on the slope.

00:09:29.268 --> 00:09:32.828
It tends to slide down the bank, so static friction acts up the bank.

00:09:33.808 --> 00:09:37.588
Above the ideal speed, the tendency reverses and friction acts down the bank.

00:09:38.208 --> 00:09:41.688
Resolve its vertical and inward components as well as the normal reaction.

00:09:42.588 --> 00:09:46.988
Do not assume N equals m g on a bank or insert mu N before checking whether

00:09:46.988 --> 00:09:47.848
friction is limiting.

00:09:48.888 --> 00:09:51.768
Wider speed range problems need those full component equations.

00:09:52.728 --> 00:09:56.768
Our toolkit now connects impulse, equilibrium, constraints and curved motion.

00:09:57.768 --> 00:10:01.389
Try the original practice questions next; they are JEE style, not claimed past

00:10:01.389 --> 00:10:01.536
paper questions.
